From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:32:48 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Relay game traffic for internet play

Local wireless play, which the room stands in for, is nearly lossless
and in order: the Wi-Fi MAC acknowledges and retransmits frames. Over
the internet the room has to choose how to relay game packets, how fast
to send them on and when to give up on a peer, and upstream's choices
suit neither case.

Sending promptly:

- The room thread handled one ENet event per enet_host_service call
  (with a 5 ms wait). The loop now handles every event ENet has already
  queued (enet_host_check_events, which does no socket I/O), flushes
  once, and then waits up to 1 ms for new traffic, so packets relayed in
  a burst go out together right away. There is still one ENet channel
  and every handler flushes as before; nothing is batched or delayed.
- The proxy and LDN handlers only read the member list, so they take a
  shared lock on member_mutex (already a shared_mutex). Relays still all
  run on the room thread (enet_peer_send is not thread-safe); this only
  stops a relay from waiting on, or blocking, other readers such as the
  announce thread.
- SendStatusMessage flushes after releasing member_mutex instead of
  doing socket I/O while holding it.
- BroadcastRoomInformation read the member count outside member_mutex
  and the members inside it, so a join or leave in between produced a
  packet whose count did not match its list. Both are read under the
  lock now.
- The nickname regex is compiled once instead of on every join request.

Delivery of game data, UDP proxy packets (EDEN_ROOM_RELAY_MODE):

- reliable: upstream's behaviour. Everything is retransmitted in order,
  so one lost packet holds up everything behind it (head-of-line
  blocking), from every sender to that member at once, since they share
  one channel. Worst on lossy, long paths; not for internet play.
- sequenced: unreliable but in order. A packet that arrives after a
  newer one is dropped, and the rule spans all senders to a member, so a
  path that reorders loses packets. Game packets also wait behind any
  lost reliable message on the channel (chat, joins, room information,
  LDN packets).
- unsequenced (the default): unreliable and unordered; each packet is
  delivered as it arrives. Games can see reordered datagrams, as they do
  online.

Unknown values log a warning and use unsequenced. The unreliable modes
also set ENET_PACKET_FLAG_UNRELIABLE_FRAGMENT, as without it ENet
silently sends packets above its fragment size (about 1366 bytes, which
large game packets exceed) as reliable fragments.

Session control is relayed reliably in every mode:

- LDN packets carry only the session's control messages (LDNPacketType:
  Scan, ScanResp, Connect, SyncNetwork, Disconnect, DestroyNetwork); game
  data travels as proxy packets. Clients send Connect, ScanResp and
  SyncNetwork once and never retry them, so one lost packet fails a join
  or a scan.
- TCP proxy packets: a stream breaks if any of it is lost or reordered.

Only the room's sending side changes: Eden clients still send to the
room reliably, and the room's own messages are always reliable.

Peer tuning, applied when a peer joins:

- ENet lowers a peer's packet throttle once its RTT rises. The throttle
  scales the reliable send window (bytes in flight) and, below its
  maximum, drops unreliable packets at random. A throttle deceleration
  of 0 keeps it at its maximum, so neither happens. ENet sends the
  setting to the client, which applies it to its own peer for the room,
  so the client's reliable uplink window is kept open too.
- Peer timeouts default to 12 s minimum and 60 s maximum instead of
  ENet's 5 s and 30 s, so a few seconds of loss on a long route do not
  drop players (EDEN_ROOM_PEER_TIMEOUT_MIN/MAX, in ms; the maximum is
  raised to the minimum if lower).
- Peers are pinged every 100 ms instead of 500 ms, which keeps RTT and
  loss statistics current (EDEN_ROOM_PING_INTERVAL, in ms).

Invalid timing values (0, non-numeric, above one hour) are ignored with a
warning.

Packets to unknown addresses: a proxy or LDN packet addressed to a fake
IP that no member holds was dropped with an error log. That happens
routinely while a session forms: a game can address a peer before the
room has assigned or announced that member's address, or keep sending to
a member that just left and rejoined with a new one, and dropping those
packets can stall a session from connecting at all. Such packets now go
to every other member, as a broadcast would, and games discard what is
not for them. EDEN_ROOM_UNKNOWN_IP_FALLBACK=drop restores upstream's
drop. The log line is debug level: it is expected traffic, and at game
packet rates the error log flooded the console.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py runs the room in unsequenced mode,
relays chat, game info, LDN and proxy traffic between members and checks
the relayed bytes are unchanged, checks relay, disconnect and reconnect,
the delivery flags members receive (UDP proxy packets unsequenced, large
ones as unreliable fragments; LDN and TCP proxy packets reliable), and
that an LDN packet to an unassigned address reaches the other member.
